                        <h3>How the Plugin Architecture Actually Works</h3>
                        <p>Imagine your ERP as a smartphone. The core is the operating system – it handles basic functions like user login, security, and database connections. Each business module (HRM, CRM, inventory) is like an app you install. You can install apps as needed, remove them, or replace them with better ones without affecting the phone's OS. That's exactly how our ERP works.</p>
                        <p>The core framework provides essential services: user management, role‑based permissions, logging, and a plugin manager. Plugins communicate with the core through well‑defined hooks (filters and actions). This means a plugin can add its own database tables, admin menus, and shortcodes, while staying completely independent.</p>

                        <h3>Shortcode Integration – The Game Changer</h3>
                        <p>One of the biggest hurdles in ERP implementation is frontend development. You need to build complex interfaces to display data from various modules. Our shortcode engine eliminates that. With a simple line like <code>[erp_attendance]</code>, you can embed an attendance dashboard on any page. Shortcodes support parameters: <code>[erp_invoice id="123" format="pdf"]</code> can even generate a PDF invoice. This approach reduces frontend development time by 70%.</p>

