        <section class="seo-section-container" id="documentation-help">
            <div class="seo-content-card animate-on-scroll">
                <h2>Final Year Project Documentation, Report, PPT & Abstract – Complete Guide</h2>
                
                <p>Panicking about documentation? Don't worry – <strong>everything is included</strong> in our package. Here's what you need to know:</p>
                
                <h3>📄 What is Project Report?</h3>
                <p>Your project report is a 50-80 page document explaining everything about your project. It follows IEEE format:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Chapter 1:</strong> Introduction & Problem Statement</li>
                    <li><strong>Chapter 2:</strong> Literature Survey (Review of existing systems)</li>
                    <li><strong>Chapter 3:</strong> System Design & Architecture</li>
                    <li><strong>Chapter 4:</strong> Implementation & Testing</li>
                    <li><strong>Chapter 5:</strong> Results & Discussion</li>
                    <li><strong>Chapter 6:</strong> Conclusion & Future Work</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>📝 What is Project Abstract?</h3>
                <p>A 150-250 word summary of your entire project. Should include:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Problem you're solving</li>
                    <li>Technology used</li>
                    <li>Key features</li>
                    <li>Results achieved</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>📊 What is Project Synopsis?</h3>
                <p>3-5 page overview submitted BEFORE starting the project. Includes:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Project title and objectives</li>
                    <li>Technology stack</li>
                    <li>Expected outcomes</li>
                    <li>Timeline</li>
                </ul>
                
                <h3>🎓 What Examiners Check in Documentation?</h3>
                <p>Examiners look for these 5 things:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Clarity:</strong> Can they understand your project?</li>
                    <li><strong>Completeness:</strong> All chapters present?</li>
                    <li><strong>Formatting:</strong> IEEE format followed?</li>
                    <li><strong>Originality:</strong> Plagiarism free?</li>
                    <li><strong>Results:</strong> Screenshots and outputs included?</li>
                </ol>
                
